troops armed with orders to shoot those  indulging in violence are patrolling sri  lanka's capital colombo today armored  personnel carriers and soldiers with  guns have been deployed to prevent  rioting and violence a state of  emergency that was to have ended today  has been extended by another day in a  country facing its worst economic crisis  in 74 years the troop presence on the  ground has raised fears of a military  takeover of the country  those soldiers on the streets a response  to this a wave of violence after  pro-government supporters attacked  peaceful demonstrators in the capital  colombo  demonstrators are demanding president  kotabia rajapaksha stepped down for his  government's failure to prevent the  economic crisis  demonstrators on tuesday beat  pro-government supporters who attacked  them with sticks and rods 104 buildings  many belonging to lawmakers from the  ruling party were torched 60 vehicles  were burnt including buses which were  used to ship pro-government supporters  to where peaceful demonstrations were  being held  at least eight people have been reported  killed including a ruling party lawmaker  an opposition politician sought to  explain what was behind the violence on  tuesday he said it has been provoked by  the government and its supporters  all protests were peaceful protests  there was no violence there was no  rioting the protests were aimed at  specific demands of the people  when former prime minister mahinda  rajapaksa launched a planned attack on  these peaceful protests he should have  known there would be a reaction to that  the crisis forced mahinda rajapaksa to  resign as prime minister at the start of  the week his brother gutabia rajapaksha  continues however as president  following tuesday's violence he tweeted  a message of unity urging all sri  lankans to join hands as one to overcome  economic social and political challenges  he also warned against sri lankans  falling prey to attempts to push them  towards racial and religious disharmony  i'll be asking an analyst in colombo  about what the president was alluding to  in that last bit but first a look at why  so much of the public anger is being  directed at the rajapaksha family  political observers have called the  rajapaksha dynasty the most unashamedly  nepotistic regime in sri lanka's history  family members held crucial ministerial  births in addition to being in  parliament and other government  departments  the family has been a part of sri lankan  politics for nearly eight decades  starting with d.a rajapaksa he was a  member of parliament right after the  country declared independence in 1948.  four of his sons followed him into  politics mahinda rajapaksa was for years  sri lanka's most powerful person during  the 2000s serving as president for a  decade and prime minister twice gotabia  served as defence minister under mahinda  and is the current president their  brothers basil and charmal also held  cabinet positions finance and irrigation  before getting sacked in the current  crisis  and many in the next generation that is  sons daughters nephews in-laws and  cousins  all found purchase in government  we can't fit them all on our chart  but here's more on sri lanka under  mahinda and gotabia rajbaksha  the streets of colombo burn with anger  against the rajapaksas  symbols of their power have come under  attack  a dramatic turn of fortune  for a family that's dominated sri lanka  politics for nearly 20 years  starting in the mid-2000s two rajapaksa  brothers rose to power as a package deal  mahinda first as prime minister  then elected as president in 2005.  he appointed his brother gotabia defense  minister  in 2009 they brutally crushed the tamil  tiger insurgency ending a decades-long  civil war  and the family brand soared  mahinda won a second term in a landslide  he packed his cabinet with family  members who used their political clout  and billions borrowed from china  to plug budget shortfalls and fund  extravagant infrastructure projects  drowning the country in debt  in 2015 mahinda lost the presidency to a  non-family cabinet member  but four years later an islamist  terrorist attack renewed public fears  over security the rajapaksas vowed to  get tough and the people voted them back  into power  again a package deal  but this time gotabia as president and  mahinda as his prime minister  but in just 30 months their grip on  power has started to unravel  experts say their lacks finance policies  have caused the island's economy to  crater  shortages of fuel food and medicine have  prompted mass protests  calls for the rajapaksa family to go  at first went unanswered  but then one by one those in government  have started to resign  including mahinda  evacuated by army commandos after angry  crowds breached the gate to his  residence  gotabia still remains  in charge of the mess of his family's  making  and joining me now for more from colombo  is ambika sadkunanathan she's  chairperson of the ngo the nilan  thiruchal trust and former commissioner  in the human rights commission of sri  lanka amica there's a lot i'd like to  get your perspective on but first  how are you coping with this crisis  i would say that probably better than  most because i think it's important to  check one's privilege and i would say in  many ways uh in comparison to the daily  wage earners for instance the farmers uh  in comparison to many of those people i  would say i'm actually doing much better  because they are in dire straits  particular communities are severely  impacted and continue to be so  particularly those whose livelihoods  depend on  the fuel for instance which has been in  shortage the kerosene which has been in  shortage  i mean we saw a lot of them attacked by  pro-government supporters  on a tuesday yet earlier today in a  tweet president gutaba rajapaksa urged  sri lankans to court reject subversive  attempts to push you towards racial and  religious disharmony what was he talking  about  good question  it's quite interesting that the  president said that considering that he  came to power on an overtly sinhala  buddhist nationalist platform that was  anti-tamil  anti-muslim and at one point he boasted  about the fact that he was elected  by the sinhala majority and therefore  for him and we have seen even since then  since he came to power how the  government tried to use religion and  ethnicity to create disharmony to create  social instability we saw that multiple  crimes even inciting hatred inciting  violence etc therefore i feel that the  president's tweet is quite hypocritical  in this instance and there are also  demands for uh president gutaba  rajapaksa to step down yet a lot of the  anger appears to also be directed  against the rajapaksa family in general  why do you think that is  absolutely it is uh the initial demand  was for the president to step down and  that has continued that demand then  evolved to asking all the raj boxes to  step down and that is because this is  and this is something that if you go to  many of the protest sites and  particularly the main the large flagship  kind of site that is a golf face  uh  they call it a family business because  uh one of the brothers the president the  other one is the prime minister several  of the other brothers are ministers  they've got their family members  relatives their in-laws appointed to  many of the positions uh they're so it's  a family business that is run in a very  non-transparent undemocratic manner and  this is something that the people have  seen how there is corruption there is  nepotism there is patronage and  public resources are used for private  purposes and this is why the the demand  is not just for the president to step  down but for the entire family who are  seen to have exploited the country for  their personal benefit  yet one can't help but notice that a lot  of the people who are out on the streets  who are protesting are the very people  who voted this family into power  repeatedly i mean a number of times the  power has alternated between mahinda  raja paksha and gotabia rajapaksa and to  go back to a point you were making  earlier this was primarily on a  sinhalese majoritarian nationalistic  platform  do you think these people are having a  change of heart is there a different  expectation that sri lankans now have of  their politicians  yes and no  i would say uh largely the demands or  the the protest at least in the south  because we must remember in the north  these protests have been taking uh you  know they have existed for a long time  the families that disappeared for  instance have been protesting peacefully  non-stop for more than thousand nine  hundred days there are constantly  protests against the military takeover  occupation and acquisition of private  land in the north and the east so those  protests have been ongoing  but i think in the south it is primarily  started out only about economics and now  it has evolved to asking for the  abolition of the executive presidency  and also  demands related to corruption once again  a lot of the there is some difference  between demands from the urban protest  as opposed to the rural protests etc but  one demand is very clear for the family  to step down in terms of the other  issues i would say particularly among  the younger people there is an awareness  or wanting to learn more about the  historical issues the ethnic conflict  etc and there finally i feel sri lankans  many of them are beginning to understand  what it means to be a citizen and to  hold your elected representatives  accountable so their notion of  citizenship is definitely evolving and  changing and they're understanding their  role or what a democracy should be those  values i feel are perhaps slowly  becoming internalized  uh  we'll have we'd love to talk more but  unfortunately we're running out of time  ambiguous  we'll have to leave it there for the  time being but thank you so much for  joining us today  thank you very much  and that's it for today as ever there's  more on our website we leave you now  with images from sri lanka where the  economic crisis has escalated troops  have shoot to kill orders a day after  protests turned violent a state of  emergency has also been extended we'll  see you back here tomorrow goodbye  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

